title: The 60-exports-correct-pattern fixture documents a stub shape the engine rejects, and it was cited as evidence twice
updated_at: "2026-08-31T04:59:59Z"
---
Found 2026-08-31 by lane-b060-b062, by game probe, while correcting a claim it
had itself committed on the strength of this fixture.

## The defect

`tests/fixtures/.../60-exports-correct-pattern` pairs

    import string SendEvent(string a, string b) from "T";        // stub
    string SendEvent(const string &in a, const string &in b)     // body

and its comment calls this the **CORRECT pattern**. It is not. Probed against
Openplanet 1.29.5 with two plugins (an exporter holding the body and declaring
the stub, and a consumer importing it):

| variant | stub vs body | engine |
|---|---|---|
| exact | `const string &in` matching the body | **binds and runs** |
| lax | `string` by value over a `const string &in` body | **`Unbound function called`** |
| control | names a function that does not exist | `Unbound function called` |

So a consumer calling the fixture's own declared shape dies at runtime. The
fixture asserts a false claim about the engine.

## Why this is worth fixing rather than tolerating

**It is load-bearing, and it has already produced two wrong conclusions.** The
lane cited it as evidence that the engine's binding is lax, and wrote that
justification into a shipped comment; the coordinator accepted the same
reasoning when approving B062's matching rule. Both were wrong, and both were
wrong *because the fixture said so*. A future lane reading it would reach the
same place.

The fixture was evidently never game-probed. That is the general hazard: **a
fixture comment asserting engine behaviour is a claim, not a measurement, and
it is trusted exactly like a measurement by everyone downstream.**

Note what caught it. The lane's first probe had only the `lax` and `control`
variants, and both failed identically — an oracle that cannot discriminate,
which it would have been entitled to read as "the engine is strict" without
having measured anything. Only adding the `exact` positive control made it a
measurement, because `exact` binds and the sole difference from `lax` is the
parameter type.

## Work

Two decisions, neither taken:

1. Whether the stub should be corrected to `const string &in a, const string
   &in b` so the fixture documents a pattern that actually binds.
2. Whether anything else asserts against its current shape. Other tests
   reference this fixture, and it is not lane-b060-b062's to change, which is
   why it was flagged rather than edited.

Prefer correcting it, and add a line to the fixture comment recording that the
shape is game-probed and on which Openplanet version — so the next reader can
tell a measured claim from an assumed one.

## Sweep, per repo CLAUDE.md

While correcting one signature, check the other export fixtures for the same
assumption rather than fixing this row alone: `&out`, non-const references,
handle parameters `T@`, `T@ &in`, defaulted parameters, and typedef'd types are
all places where a stub could differ from its body and where nothing currently
establishes what the engine does.

## Related

B062 (whose matching rule is a deliberate under-approximation and is unaffected
either way — it matches on name and arity, never on types), B084,
`docs/verification-hazards.md`.